>
  <h2>Why chocolate hurts dogs but not usually people</h2>
  <p>
    Chocolate comes from cacao seeds rich in methylxanthines, mainly theobromine and some caffeine.
    Humans metabolize theobromine relatively quickly. Dogs process it slowly, so it builds up to levels
    that overstimulate the heart and nervous system. The FDA’s consumer update on chocolate and dogs
    notes that even small amounts can cause vomiting and diarrhea, while larger amounts can produce
    tremors, irregular heartbeat, seizures, and death. There is no safe “treat” amount for dogs; chocolate
    belongs in the human snack cabinet, not the treat pouch.
  </p>

  <h2>Not all chocolate is equal</h2>
  <p>
    Theobromine content rises with cocoa percentage. Unsweetened baking chocolate and high-percentage
    dark bars can carry 400 mg or more of theobromine per ounce. Milk chocolate might contain roughly
    50 to 60 mg per ounce, and white chocolate traces only. Cocoa powder used in brownies is extremely
    concentrated because fat has been removed, so a spilled canister is more dangerous than it looks.
    Holiday assortments mix types; always identify the worst-case piece your dog could have swallowed.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Product (approx.)</th><th>Theobromine risk</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cocoa powder</td><td>Very high</td><td>Used in baking; small volumes add up fast.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Baking chocolate</td><td>Very high</td><td>One square can threaten small dogs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dark chocolate (70%+)</td><td>High</td><td>Popular gifts; keep off coffee tables.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Milk chocolate</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Large dogs may tolerate tiny amounts; still call if unsure.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>White chocolate</td><td>Very low theobromine</td><td>Still fatty; pancreatitis possible.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <a href="/tools/dog-chocolate-toxicity-calculator/">dog chocolate toxicity calculator</a> combines
    your dog’s weight, the chocolate type, and ounces eaten to estimate milligrams of theobromine per
    kilogram and suggest whether monitoring at home, an urgent vet visit, or poison hotline contact is
    appropriate. It is a triage aid, not a diagnosis; always confirm with a professional when exposure is
    real.
  </p>

  <h2>How poisoning unfolds</h2>
  <p>
    Absorption begins within an hour. Mild cases show stomach upset and hyperactivity. Moderate cases
    may add panting, pacing, and a pounding pulse you can feel in the chest. Severe cases progress to
    muscle tremors, fever, red gums, irregular rhythm, seizures, and coma. The Merck Veterinary Manual
    describes symptoms lasting 12 to 36 hours because theobromine recirculates through the liver and
    intestines. Older dogs, those with heart disease, and very small dogs tolerate less before crisis.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chocolate with fillings adds other risks: xylitol-sweetened candy is acutely toxic to dogs, raisins
    in bars cause kidney failure, and macadamia nuts trigger neurologic signs. Tell poison control
    everything in the box, not just the chocolate shell.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do in the first ten minutes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Remove remaining chocolate and keep the wrapper; note the product and estimated ounces missing.</li>
    <li>Weigh your dog if possible, or recall the last vet weight.</li>
    <li>Run the calculator and call your veterinarian or ASPCA Animal Poison Control at <a href="tel:+18884264435">(888) 426-4435</a> or <a href="tel:+18557647661">Pet Poison Helpline (855) 764-7661</a>.</li>
    <li>Do not give human medications, charcoal, or home remedies unless instructed.</li>
    <li>Transport to an emergency clinic if advised, bringing wrappers and timing details.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    ASPCA Poison Control and the Pet Poison Helpline employ veterinary toxicologists who calculate
    exposure daily. A consultation fee applies, but the guidance prevents both under- and over-treatment.
    If your dog is already vomiting, tremoring, or collapsing, skip online research and go straight to
    emergency care while someone else calls ahead.
  </p>

  <h2>What veterinarians may do</h2>
  <p>
    For recent ingestions, clinics may induce vomiting if the dog is alert and the chocolate was eaten
    within a few hours. Activated charcoal may reduce further absorption. IV fluids support the kidneys
    and blood pressure. Heart monitoring, sedatives for agitation, and anti-seizure drugs treat severe
    cases. There is no universal antidote; treatment is supportive until theobromine clears. Dogs with
    pre-existing arrhythmias may stay hospitalized longer on ECG monitoring.
  </p>

  <h2>Prevention beats panic</h2>
  <p>
    Dogs are opportunists; counters and purses are not secure. During holidays, put gift bags in closets,
    empty cocoa powder into high cabinets, and teach children not to share Halloween haul. Crate training
    helps when baking. Remind guests that sugar-free desserts may contain xylitol. AVMA household hazard
    lists include chocolate alongside grapes, onions, and medications; post the list on the fridge.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you bake with dogs in the kitchen, sweep floors before releasing them; a single dropped chip of
    dark chocolate matters for a 5 lb Yorkie. Trash cans with flip lids fail against determined Labs;
    use latching bins.
  </p>

  <h2>Special cases</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pregnant or nursing dogs</strong> should be treated aggressively because fetal stress complicates
    care. <strong>Dogs on MAOI drugs or certain behavior medications</strong> may have exaggerated
    stimulant effects; mention all prescriptions to poison control. <strong>Repeated small exposures</strong>
    over a week—stealing from a kid’s backpack—can accumulate; chronic low-grade vomiting warrants a
    diet history review even if no single binge occurred.
  </p>

  <h2>After the scare</h2>
  <p>
    Follow discharge instructions on diet and activity. Some dogs need bland meals for a day after
    vomiting. Schedule a recheck if heart arrhythmia was noted.     Use the episode to update your emergency
    kit with poison numbers, your dog’s weight written in pen, and a plan for who drives to the clinic at
    night. Pair this guide with the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/dog-chocolate-toxicity-calculator/">dog chocolate toxicity calculator</a> whenever
    exposure happens, and with routine care tools such as the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/dog-bmi-calculator/">dog BMI and body condition helper</a> and the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/dog-food-calculator/">dog food calculator</a> for everyday calories, so treats never
    need to come from the candy drawer.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Chocolate and dogs do not mix. Theobromine toxicity is dose-dependent and unpredictable at home, so
    treat every ingestion seriously, estimate risk with the calculator, and involve ASPCA Poison Control
    or your veterinarian immediately for moderate or unknown amounts. Prevention and fast professional
    guidance save lives; waiting until tremors start turns a manageable call into a critical hospital stay.
  </p>
